What is the purpose of a stack in computer science?

A **stack** is an abstract data type (ADT) that follows the Last-In-First-Out (LIFO, pronounced "last-in-first-out") principle. This means that elements are added and removed from one end called the top of the stack.

In simpler terms:

1. The last element pushed onto the stack will be the first to come out.
2. Elements can only be inserted or extracted at this single point—the 'top' of the stack.

Here's how you might implement stacks using different approaches: 

### Using Dart List as Stack

```dart
class MyStack {
  final _list = <dynamic>[];

  void push(dynamic item){
    // Add new items on the list which acts like our stack 
    _list.add(item);
   }

  dynamic pop(){
     if (_list.isEmpty)
       throw Exception('Cannot Pop From Empty Stack');

      return _list.removeLast();

}

  bool isEmpty() => _list.isEmpty;
}
```
This code creates `MyStack` class with methods for pushing (`push`) adding value into it; popping(`pop`, removing & returning most recently added), checking whether empty(`isEmpty`). Note here we use standard library collection i.e., lists but behave them according LIFO principles.


